WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:05.480
North American forests looked very different 300 years ago.

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:10.540
And it’s not just the rise of infrastructure – it’s the purposeful decline of many natural predators.

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:15.780
Before European colonists came to America, the gray wolf population looked something like this:

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:20.850
looked something like this.
But by the 1930s, that vast, thriving

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:25.890
population looked more like this. In a
matter of a few decades European

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:30.269
settlers had trapped, poisoned, and shot
the gray wolf nearly out of existence in

00:00:30.269 --> 00:00:33.930
the lower 48.
Today, that map looks a little more like this.

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:38.730
The gray wolf has been on the
endangered species list since 1974 and

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:42.300
those decades of restoration efforts
have recovered the population to a few

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:46.280
key locations, though nowhere near where it used to be.

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:50.820
But in 2019 the Fish and Wildlife Service filed this – It's a proposal to remove the animal from the

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:55.079
endangered species list nationwide,
arguing that the gray wolf is no longer

00:00:55.079 --> 00:00:58.980
threatened. A hundred scientists
responded with a letter, urging the Fish

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:04.400
and Wildlife Service to rescind the
proposal saying, "it's way too soon."

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:09.049
So, when are we done protecting the gray
wolf?

00:01:11.229 --> 00:01:16.790
In 1973 President Nixon signed the
Endangered Species Act. It allows the

00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:19.970
Fish &amp; Wildlife Service to protect
certain species from extinction by

00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:23.270
limiting hunting, trapping, and killing.
When the government decides that a

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:26.000
species is sufficiently recovered,
they're removed from the endangered

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:30.439
species list or "delisted" and they
release management of the species back

00:01:30.439 --> 00:01:33.740
to the States. Unsurprisingly this is a lot more

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:35.520
complicated than it sounds.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:37.720
The federal government is pretty efficient at

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:40.280
listing species when it's the right
thing to do

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:42.120
but delisting that's different.

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:46.800
This is John Vucetich who studies wildlife ecology at Michigan Tech University.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:49.520
In the entire 40-plus years of the

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:54.619
Endangered Species Act there have only
been in the neighborhood of a dozen

00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:58.020
delistings. We're far less experienced at
that.

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:02.040
The Fish and Wildlife Service has tried to delist the gray wolf in certain states before,

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:05.860
and was met with a bunch of lawsuits from conservationists and environmental groups.

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:09.760
The reason it's so challenging to delist an animal like the gray wolf

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:12.140
lies in the law itself.

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:15.280
The easy way to understand the
obligations of the Endangered Species

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:21.410
Act is that a species is recovered and
no longer requires federal protection

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:23.620
when it's no longer at risk of
extinction.

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:26.920
So let's zoom out on that map from earlier with this in mind.

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:30.040
The gray wolf is not at risk of extinction.

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:34.940
There have always been a whole bunch of wolves in Canada and in Russia. So if we lost

00:02:34.940 --> 00:02:37.960
every single wolf in all of the United
States even Alaska

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:40.420
they're not at risk of extinction.

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:42.500
But the thing is the Endangered
Species Act is narrower than that.

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:47.389
The legal definition of an
endangered species is "one that is at

00:02:47.389 --> 00:02:52.620
risk of extinction throughout all or a
significant portion of its range."

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:57.060
This is the phrase that has been heavily debated in court over the gray wolf.

00:02:57.060 --> 00:03:01.500
Because while somewhere around 6,000 wolves may roam here many more used to roam here.

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:05.240
Meaning the animal only exists in about 15% of its former range.

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:09.960
So in order to delist the gray wolf we have to decide if that 15% is enough.

00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:16.040
The Endangered Species Act isn't clear
on how to define "range."

00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:18.840
When it comes to the gray wolf, the Fish and Wildlife Service argues that the law could mean

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:22.890
"current range." They're satisfied with
that 15%, especially because of how tough

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:26.460
it would be to reintroduce the wolf to
all places it used to roam.

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:30.600
Many sections of their former range are no longer suitable due to human encroachment.

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:36.020
And in places where wolves do coexist with people living alongside them is challenging.

00:03:36.020 --> 00:03:40.200
I mean, wolves are happy to
kill a cow or sheep if that's what it

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:47.310
needs to do in order to live and get by,
and of course that's tough – or can be

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:49.216
tough – for the livestock owner.

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:52.340
Regardless, environmentalists interpret this law differently.

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:54.960
They argue that the wolves range should be interpreted

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:58.740
historically, and that until it's
recovered in most of its historic

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:01.500
territory it should fall into the
protections of the Endangered Species Act

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:06.840
It's this tension the law that makes
delisting hard to figure out.

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:10.620
Still, wolves have been successfully removed from the endangered species list

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:14.180
in Idaho in Montana in 2011 and in Wyoming in 2017.

00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:17.600
The states are now in charge of their management and making sure

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:20.360
they retain a certain population. So in Idaho

00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:24.000
for example, it's legal to hunt wolves
with a permit but the state has promised

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:29.400
to keep the population at or over 15
packs. Under Idaho's law wolves do stay

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:34.530
alive, but they don't have room to branch
out to new or historic territories where

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:38.130
they might thrive, which is likely what
will happen if they're delisted across

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:42.300
the U.S. The current population will
remain stable, but the wolves will only

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:48.270
exist where they are right now –
not where they could be or have been.

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:53.640
The endangered species act is pretty clear that when a species is endangered it does not

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:58.919
matter if we can find why that species
is valuable or not. The species is

00:04:58.919 --> 00:05:00.919
valuable – according to the Endangered
Species Act – just because.

00:05:00.919 --> 00:05:06.960
but figuring on a balance between protecting a species and thriving alongside them is tough –

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:11.740
especially when that species is a long-hated predator.

